# `fluid::network`

Validates a description and compiles it into a `Network`.

## Signature

```cpp
Network network(const Net &net, const Liquid &liquid = {});
```

## What It Does

Checks the description for consistency and builds the constant topology maps once: the node-pipe incidence, the selection of the variable-head nodes, and the device masks. Host work is limited to integer connectivity checks; every physical array stays a `Series`.

## Parameters

`net`
: The `Net` to compile.

`liquid`
: The `Liquid`; water at 20 °C by default.

## Returns

An immutable `Network`.

## Errors

Throws `std::invalid_argument` for a malformed description: a component with no reservoir or tank, an endpoint outside the range, a self-loop, non-positive dimensions, a demand at a reservoir, non-floating or non-finite values, mixed precision or platform, and leading axes that disagree.

## Interpretation and Use

Compile once and solve many times. The error text names the rule that was broken. Compilation does not solve anything, so a description that compiles can still fail in `steady`, for example for a setting the network cannot satisfy.

